This PR prints original output when the test are failed and fixes the issue by using `std::cerr` not `std::clog`

Note: The issue seems to highly depend on the runtime and logic from mpi. In the end, I will provide one reproducible code, which have higher chance to lead the problem on my side.

Issue (Unfortunately, I can not really find a resource describing the behavior):
MPI collects all output from all ranks and then forward them into the terminal (or the session on the mpirun lancher). When collecting the information from clog and cout, there is no clear or deterministic order from clog or cout even if they are from the same rank.  
clog writes the information into buffer for stderr. If the clog message is not flushed but  cout triggers the flush, the messages include clog's part are all flushed into stdout.

The issue can be seen in https://gitlab.com/ginkgo-project/ginkgo-public-ci/-/jobs/11883585493
"Running spmv: csr-csr" is shown before the original output without linebreak.
However, we actually flush right after the output by `std::endl` in 
https://github.com/ginkgo-project/ginkgo/blob/d855ff4a8a4c3b00e6f310dec385d538f288af84/benchmark/utils/runner.hpp#L135-L136
I guess MPI runtime does not always put them together.

To workaround that, I hope avoiding buffer in clog or just using cerr to solve this issue.
I do not find the behavior resource and it is race condition problem, so hard to say it is indeed the issue and the patch really fix it.

If someone has the information for this kind of behavior and share, it will be great and useful.


The reproducer (openmpi v5.0.7 and gcc v13.3.0)
```
#include <mpi.h>
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>
#include <chrono>

int main(int argc, char** argv) {
    MPI_Init(&argc, &argv);
    int rank;
    MPI_Comm_rank(MPI_COMM_WORLD, &rank);

    if (rank == 0) {
        // clog might not be in stdout (which should not)
        std::clog << "This is buffered clog 1";
        std::cout << "This is buffered cout 1" << std::endl;
        std::clog << "This is buffered clog 2" << std::endl;

        // clog might be in stdout with delay
        std::clog << "This is buffered clog 3";
        std::cout << "This is buffered cout 3" << std::endl;
        std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(5000));
        std::clog << "This is buffered clog 4" << std::endl;

        // Unbuffered cerr
        std::cerr << "This is unbuffered cerr\n";

    }

    MPI_Finalize();
    return 0;
}
```
